--TEST--
parsing a file is `Core\IO::read` composed with `Core\Ast::parse`, so the `fs.read` check is the IO door's and parsing itself asks for nothing — `rule:core-classes/ast-is-inert`, `rule:security/reflection-needs-no-capability`, `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// There is no `Core\Ast::parseFile`. A path is the filesystem's question and
// `Core\IO` is the one door that asks it, so parsing a file is two calls and
// the grant is checked in the place it already lived -- `Core\Ast` stays
// capability-free (`rule:security/reflection-needs-no-capability`).
Core\IO::write("subject.nvs", "<?nvs echo 1 + 2;\n");
var $tree = Core\Ast::parse(Core\IO::read("subject.nvs"));
echo $tree->kind(), " holds ", Core\Arr::count($tree->nodes()), " nodes\n";

// One grammar, one answer: the text read from the file and the same text a
// program already holds parse to the same tree, which is what makes the
// composition a spelling rather than a second path
// (`rule:core-classes/ast-is-inert`).
var $inline = Core\Ast::parse("<?nvs echo 1 + 2;\n");
bool $agreed = Core\Arr::count($inline->nodes()) == Core\Arr::count($tree->nodes());
echo "the file and the same text agree: ", $agreed ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The bound, on the door's side: a path the grant does not cover is refused
// before anything is read, so `parse` is never reached and there is no second
// place the check could have been forgotten
// (`rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`).
try {
    Core\Ast::parse(Core\IO::read("../outside.nvs"));
    echo "read ../outside.nvs: parsed\n";
} catch (IOError $touched) {
    echo "read ../outside.nvs: the os answered first\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "read ../outside.nvs: refused at the door\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
File holds 4 nodes
the file and the same text agree: yes
read ../outside.nvs: refused at the door
